 5.6 points, when the 14k played G3 and lost 18.2 points. That moved the lead from -5.6 to +12.6 for Opus 5.5 and was the first big swing. The engine's choice was G5, which Opus 5.5 took on the next move."}, {"turn": 23, "text": "Opus 5.5 played G1 and lost 10.4 points, dropping the lead from +5.0 to -5.4. The engine preferred E7, and Opus 5.5 played E7 two turns later, when it held the lead at +0.2."}, {"turn": 39, "text": "The 14k had just played C9 at move 38, losing 23.7 points and putting Black at +19.9. Opus 5.5 answered with B9, losing 23.5 points and sliding to -3.6. The engine wanted H8 here. Move 37, C8, had already cost 17.8 points, so this corner was costly for Opus 5.5 twice."}, {"turn": 40, "text": "The 14k played D9 and lost 23.5 points, which took the lead from -3.6 back to +19.9 for Black. This was the largest swing of the game, and it settled the result. The engine's choice was H8, the point Opus 5.5 took next."}, {"turn": 41, "text": "Opus 5.5 found H8, the engine's own choice, and lost only 0.1 points. After this the lead barely moved, staying near +20 through the quiet endgame to the final B+20."}]}, "audit": {"by": "Sonnet 5.5", "verdict": "clean", "reason": "libs.py flood-fills each group on a pasted board and prints its stones and liberties, flagging groups with 2 or fewer. count.py counts stones and enclosed empty regions for area scoring on the current position. Neither proposes, ranks, or looks ahead at moves, so both only inspect the current position.", "stop": false}}